body:
## Why arrays and objects matter

So far, you have worked mostly with individual values such as strings, numbers, and booleans. Real applications usually need to work with collections of related data.

A webshop cart is a good example. One product might be represented by an object:

~~~js
const item = {
  name: "Keyboard",
  price: 49.99,
  quantity: 2
};
~~~

A shopping cart contains multiple items, so an **array** is a natural choice:

~~~js
const cart = [
  { name: "Keyboard", price: 49.99, quantity: 2 },
  { name: "Mouse", price: 24.99, quantity: 1 },
  { name: "Monitor", price: 199.99, quantity: 1 }
];
~~~

An object describes one thing. An array stores an ordered collection of things.

You will use this combination constantly when working with React and APIs.

## Creating and reading arrays

An array is created using square brackets.

~~~js
const products = ["Keyboard", "Mouse", "Monitor"];
~~~

Each item has an **index**, which is its numeric position in the array. JavaScript arrays start at index `0`.

~~~js
console.log(products[0]); // "Keyboard"
console.log(products[1]); // "Mouse"
~~~

The `length` property tells you how many items the array contains.

~~~js
console.log(products.length); // 3
~~~

You can read the last item using the last index, although modern JavaScript also provides `at()`.

~~~js
console.log(products[products.length - 1]);
console.log(products.at(-1));
~~~

An array can contain any JavaScript values, including objects and even other arrays.

~~~js
const numbers = [10, 20, 30];
const mixed = ["hello", 42, true, null];
~~~

In application code, arrays are often homogeneous in practice: a cart is normally an array of cart item objects, for example.

## Adding and removing items

The `push()` method adds an item to the end of an array.

~~~js
const cart = ["Keyboard", "Mouse"];
cart.push("Monitor");

console.log(cart);
// ["Keyboard", "Mouse", "Monitor"]
~~~

`pop()` removes and returns the last item.

~~~js
const lastItem = cart.pop();
~~~

`unshift()` adds an item at the beginning.

~~~js
cart.unshift("USB cable");
~~~

`shift()` removes and returns the first item.

~~~js
const firstItem = cart.shift();
~~~

These methods **mutate** the array. Mutation means changing the existing value instead of creating a new one.

That distinction becomes important when you start working with React state.

## Reading objects

Objects store named properties.

~~~js
const product = {
  name: "Keyboard",
  price: 49.99,
  quantity: 2
};
~~~

You can access a property with dot notation:

~~~js
product.name;
product.price;
~~~

You can also use bracket notation:

~~~js
product["name"];
~~~

Bracket notation becomes especially useful when the property name is stored in a variable.

~~~js
const key = "price";
console.log(product[key]);
~~~

You can add or update a property by assigning to it.

~~~js
product.quantity = 3;
product.inStock = true;
~~~

You can remove a property with `delete`.

~~~js
delete product.inStock;
~~~

In React applications, however, you will often create a new object rather than directly modifying an existing one.

## for...of

The `for...of` loop is a straightforward way to process every value in an iterable such as an array.

~~~js
const products = ["Keyboard", "Mouse", "Monitor"];

for (const product of products) {
  console.log(product);
}
~~~

For a cart, you could calculate the number of products like this:

~~~js
let count = 0;

for (const item of cart) {
  count += item.quantity;
}
~~~

`for...of` is often a good choice when the logic is procedural and you want to perform several operations inside the same loop.

## map: transform every item

`map()` creates a new array by transforming every item in the original array.

~~~diagram
{"type": "flow", "title": "A data pipeline", "caption": "Each step returns a new array, so you can chain them — the original cart is never changed.", "nodes": [{"id": "a", "label": "cart", "sub": "5 items", "kind": "code"}, {"id": "f", "label": ".filter()", "sub": "inStock → 4"}, {"id": "m", "label": ".map()", "sub": "price × qty"}, {"id": "r", "label": ".reduce()", "sub": "sum"}, {"id": "t", "label": "27 990 Ft", "highlight": true}], "edges": [{"from": "a", "to": "f"}, {"from": "f", "to": "m", "label": "4 items"}, {"from": "m", "to": "r", "label": "[numbers]"}, {"from": "r", "to": "t"}]}
~~~

~~~js
const prices = [10, 20, 30];
const doubled = prices.map(price => price * 2);

console.log(doubled);
// [20, 40, 60]
~~~

The original array is not changed.

For objects, `map()` is useful when you need a modified representation of every item.

~~~js
const names = cart.map(item => item.name);
~~~

Now `names` contains only the product names.

You can also create a new object for each item:

~~~js
const withTotals = cart.map(item => ({
  ...item,
  lineTotal: item.price * item.quantity
}));
~~~

## filter: keep matching items

`filter()` creates a new array containing only the items for which the callback returns `true`.

~~~js
const expensive = cart.filter(item => item.price > 50);
~~~

For example, a webshop could show only products that are currently in stock:

~~~js
const available = products.filter(product => product.inStock);
~~~

If nothing matches, `filter()` returns an empty array.

## find: get one matching item

`find()` returns the first item that matches a condition.

~~~js
const keyboard = cart.find(item => item.name === "Keyboard");
~~~

If there is no match, `find()` returns `undefined`.

This is useful when you expect one item, such as finding a product by its ID.

~~~js
const product = products.find(product => product.id === 42);
~~~

## some and every

`some()` answers the question: **does at least one item match?**

~~~js
const hasExpensiveItem = cart.some(item => item.price > 100);
~~~

`every()` answers a different question: **do all items match?**

~~~js
const allInStock = cart.every(item => item.inStock);
~~~

These methods return booleans, making them useful in conditions.

~~~js
if (cart.some(item => item.quantity > 5)) {
  console.log("Large quantity detected");
}
~~~

## reduce: calculate one result

`reduce()` processes an array and combines its items into a single result.

For example, calculate the total quantity in a cart:

~~~js
const count = cart.reduce(
  (total, item) => total + item.quantity,
  0
);
~~~

The second argument, `0`, is the **initial value** of the accumulator.

You can calculate a cart total in the same way:

~~~js
const total = cart.reduce(
  (sum, item) => sum + item.price * item.quantity,
  0
);
~~~

`reduce()` is powerful, but do not use it just because you can. If a simple `for...of` loop makes complicated logic clearer, the loop can be the better choice.

## sort and its compare function

`sort()` orders an array. One important detail is that `sort()` **mutates the original array**.

Without a compare function, JavaScript sorts values as strings, which can produce surprising results for numbers.

~~~js
const numbers = [10, 2, 30, 4];
numbers.sort();

console.log(numbers);
// [10, 2, 30, 4] in lexicographic order
~~~

For numeric sorting, provide a compare function.

~~~js
numbers.sort((a, b) => a - b);
~~~

For descending order:

~~~js
numbers.sort((a, b) => b - a);
~~~

With objects, you can sort by a property:

~~~js
cart.sort((a, b) => a.price - b.price);
~~~

Remember that this changes `cart` itself. If you need a sorted copy, copy first.

~~~js
const sortedCart = [...cart].sort((a, b) => a.price - b.price);
~~~

## includes

`includes()` checks whether an array contains a specific value.

~~~js
const categories = ["books", "games", "electronics"];

categories.includes("games");
// true

categories.includes("clothing");
// false
~~~

It is particularly convenient for simple membership checks.

## Destructuring arrays and objects

**Destructuring** lets you extract values from arrays or objects into variables.

For an array:

~~~js
const coordinates = [47.5, 19.0];
const [latitude, longitude] = coordinates;
~~~

For an object:

~~~js
const product = {
  name: "Keyboard",
  price: 49.99
};

const { name, price } = product;
~~~

This is common in React because component props are often destructured directly.

~~~jsx
function ProductCard({ name, price }) {
  return <div>{name}: {price} EUR</div>;
}
~~~

You can rename a destructured property:

~~~js
const { name: productName } = product;
~~~

You can also provide a default value.

~~~js
const { quantity = 1 } = product;
~~~

## Spread syntax

The **spread syntax** uses `...` to expand values into a new array or object.

To copy an array:

~~~js
const copy = [...cart];
~~~

To merge arrays:

~~~js
const first = [1, 2];
const second = [3, 4];
const merged = [...first, ...second];
~~~

For objects:

~~~js
const productWithStock = {
  ...product,
  inStock: true
};
~~~

If the same property appears more than once, the later value wins.

~~~js
const updatedProduct = {
  ...product,
  price: 39.99
};
~~~

Spread creates a **shallow copy**. This means the outer array or object is new, but nested objects are still shared references unless you copy those too.

## Immutability and React

**Immutability** means treating an existing value as something you do not modify directly. Instead, you create a new value containing the desired changes.

~~~compare
{"left": {"title": "Mutating", "lang": "js", "code": "cart.items.push(item);\nsetCart(cart); // same object", "note": "React sees the same reference and may skip the update."}, "right": {"title": "New copy", "lang": "js", "code": "setCart({\n  ...cart,\n  items: [...cart.items, item],\n});", "note": "A new object → React knows something changed."}}
~~~

Consider a cart array:

~~~js
const cart = [
  { name: "Keyboard", quantity: 1 }
];
~~~

A direct mutation would be:

~~~js
cart[0].quantity = 2;
~~~

Instead, you can create a new array and a new object:

~~~js
const updatedCart = cart.map(item =>
  item.name === "Keyboard"
    ? { ...item, quantity: 2 }
    : item
);
~~~

This approach is important in React because React uses changes in state values to determine when the UI needs to update. Creating new arrays and objects also makes state changes easier to reason about.

When you eventually use React state, you will commonly see code like this:

~~~jsx
setCart(currentCart => [
  ...currentCart,
  newItem
]);
~~~

Instead of changing the existing state array, this creates a new one.

## JSON.stringify and JSON.parse

**JSON**, or JavaScript Object Notation, is a text format commonly used to exchange data between applications.

`JSON.stringify()` converts a JavaScript value into JSON text.

~~~js
const product = {
  name: "Keyboard",
  price: 49.99
};

const json = JSON.stringify(product);
console.log(json);
~~~

`JSON.parse()` performs the opposite operation: it converts valid JSON text into a JavaScript value.

~~~js
const product = JSON.parse(json);
console.log(product.name);
~~~

This is important when working with HTTP APIs. A Spring Boot backend might return JSON representing products, tasks, or bookings, which your React frontend then works with as JavaScript objects.

JSON is text, so it cannot represent every JavaScript value directly. For example, functions are not JSON data, and special JavaScript values such as `undefined` have special serialization behaviour.

## Object.keys, Object.values and Object.entries

JavaScript provides useful methods for inspecting object properties.

`Object.keys()` returns an array of property names.

~~~js
const product = {
  name: "Keyboard",
  price: 49.99,
  quantity: 2
};

Object.keys(product);
// ["name", "price", "quantity"]
~~~

`Object.values()` returns the property values.

~~~js
Object.values(product);
// ["Keyboard", 49.99, 2]
~~~

`Object.entries()` returns an array containing `[key, value]` pairs.

~~~js
Object.entries(product);
~~~

You can loop through those entries:

~~~js
for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(product)) {
  console.log(key, value);
}
~~~

This is useful when you do not know the property names in advance.

## A complete cart example

Let's combine the main tools into one example.

~~~js
const cart = [
  { name: "Keyboard", price: 49.99, quantity: 2 },
  { name: "Mouse", price: 24.99, quantity: 1 },
  { name: "Monitor", price: 199.99, quantity: 1 }
];

const count = cart.reduce(
  (total, item) => total + item.quantity,
  0
);

const total = cart.reduce(
  (sum, item) => sum + item.price * item.quantity,
  0
);

const mostExpensive = cart.reduce((current, item) => {
  if (!current || item.price > current.price) {
    return item;
  }
  return current;
}, null);

console.log(count);
console.log(total);
console.log(mostExpensive);
~~~

The important part is not memorising every method. It is learning to recognise the type of operation you need: transform with `map`, filter with `filter`, find one with `find`, check conditions with `some` or `every`, and combine values with `reduce`.

## Common mistakes

### Forgetting that sort mutates

This changes the original array:

~~~js
items.sort((a, b) => a.price - b.price);
~~~

If the original must remain unchanged, copy first:

~~~js
const sorted = [...items].sort((a, b) => a.price - b.price);
~~~

### Mutating React-style state

Avoid directly changing an array or object that represents application state.

Instead of:

~~~js
cart.push(newItem);
~~~

create a new array:

~~~js
const updatedCart = [...cart, newItem];
~~~

### Forgetting that spread is shallow

This does not deeply clone nested objects.

~~~js
const copy = { ...product };
~~~

If `product.details` is an object, both objects still reference the same `details` object.

### Confusing map and filter

Use `map()` when you want one output item for each input item. Use `filter()` when you want to keep only some input items.

~~~js
const names = cart.map(item => item.name);
const expensive = cart.filter(item => item.price > 100);
~~~

### Using find when you need every match

`find()` returns only the first matching item. If you need all matching items, use `filter()`.

## What you should remember

Arrays and objects form the basic data structures you will use throughout the rest of the course. A shopping cart is simply an array of objects, but the same pattern applies to tasks, users, products, bookings, comments, and API responses.

The most important skill is choosing the right operation and keeping data changes predictable. In React, this becomes especially important because state updates should normally produce new arrays or objects instead of mutating existing ones.

Once these operations become familiar, working with API data becomes much easier. A response from Spring Boot will typically become a JavaScript object or array, and your React code will transform that data into the interface the user sees.

